{"subjectId":"sql","id":"set-operations","title":"UNION, INTERSECT & EXCEPT","group":"Joins & Set Operations","order":31,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Set operators combine the <strong>results of two queries</strong> vertically (more rows), unlike joins which combine tables horizontally (more columns).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Returns</th><th>Duplicates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>UNION</code></td><td>Rows in either query</td><td>Removed</td></tr><tr><td><code>UNION ALL</code></td><td>Rows in either query</td><td>Kept (faster — no de-dup step)</td></tr><tr><td><code>INTERSECT</code></td><td>Rows in both queries</td><td>Removed</td></tr><tr><td><code>EXCEPT</code> (<code>MINUS</code> in Oracle)</td><td>Rows in the first but not the second</td><td>Removed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules: both queries must return the <strong>same number of columns</strong> with compatible types; column names come from the first query; one <code>ORDER BY</code> at the very end sorts the combined result.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layers-union\"></i>UNION vs UNION ALL</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- cities we have customers or offices in\nSELECT city FROM customers WHERE city IS NOT NULL\nUNION\nSELECT location FROM departments\nORDER BY 1;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>city\n---------\nBengaluru\nDelhi\nHyderabad\nMumbai\nPune</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT city FROM customers WHERE city IS NOT NULL\nUNION ALL\nSELECT location FROM departments\nORDER BY 1;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>city\n---------\nBengaluru\nBengaluru\nDelhi\nDelhi\nHyderabad\nMumbai\nMumbai\nPune</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>UNION ALL</code> unless you actually need duplicates removed — <code>UNION</code> has to sort or hash the whole result to de-duplicate.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-layers-intersect-2\"></i>INTERSECT and EXCEPT</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- cities with both a customer and an office\nSELECT city FROM customers\nINTERSECT\nSELECT location FROM departments\nORDER BY 1;\n\n-- office cities with no customers\nSELECT location FROM departments\nEXCEPT\nSELECT city FROM customers\nORDER BY 1;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>city\n---------\nBengaluru\nMumbai\n\nlocation\n---------\nHyderabad\nPune</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Set operators compare <code>NULL</code>s as equal (unlike <code>=</code>), so <code>EXCEPT</code> and <code>INTERSECT</code> are also a null-safe way to diff two result sets. MySQL supports <code>INTERSECT</code>/<code>EXCEPT</code> only since 8.0.31; Oracle calls <code>EXCEPT</code> <code>MINUS</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tags\"></i>Labelled union: combining different sources</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT 'employee' AS kind, name, dept_id AS ref FROM employees WHERE salary &gt;= 110000\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'customer', name, customer_id FROM customers WHERE city = 'Delhi'\nORDER BY kind, name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>kind     | name  | ref\n---------+-------+----\ncustomer | Imran | 4\ncustomer | Rahul | 2\nemployee | Asha  | 1\nemployee | Priya | 2\nemployee | Ravi  | 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-compare\"></i>Comparing two tables</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE prices_old (sku TEXT, price INTEGER);\nCREATE TABLE prices_new (sku TEXT, price INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO prices_old VALUES ('A', 10), ('B', 20), ('C', 30);\nINSERT INTO prices_new VALUES ('A', 10), ('B', 25), ('D', 40);\n\nSELECT 'removed/changed' AS diff, * FROM (SELECT * FROM prices_old EXCEPT SELECT * FROM prices_new)\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'added/changed', * FROM (SELECT * FROM prices_new EXCEPT SELECT * FROM prices_old)\nORDER BY sku, diff;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>diff            | sku | price\n----------------+-----+------\nadded/changed   | B   | 25\nremoved/changed | B   | 20\nremoved/changed | C   | 30\nadded/changed   | D   | 40</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between UNION and UNION ALL?</summary><div><p><code>UNION</code> removes duplicate rows from the combined result (extra sort/hash work); <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps every row and is faster. Use <code>UNION ALL</code> when the inputs can't overlap or duplicates are wanted.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between UNION and JOIN?</summary><div><p><code>UNION</code> stacks the rows of two compatible queries (same columns) — the result has more rows. <code>JOIN</code> matches rows of two tables on a condition — the result has more columns.</p></div></details></section>"}